1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,323
Here's how to reverse engineer Sortly from a written spec.

2
00:00:03,373 --> 00:00:07,250
Claude passed all twenty-one checks, and found sixty-three gaps in my spec.

3
00:00:08,117 --> 00:00:09,909
We gave Claude one Markdown file.

4
00:00:10,079 --> 00:00:13,239
It copies the part of Sortly a small team actually uses.

5
00:00:13,498 --> 00:00:15,930
Quantities for every item in every location.

6
00:00:16,314 --> 00:00:18,000
QR labels you scan with a phone.

7
00:00:18,234 --> 00:00:19,365
Low stock alerts.

8
00:00:19,600 --> 00:00:21,114
Purchase orders you receive against.

9
00:00:21,434 --> 00:00:22,667
And blind stock counts.

10
00:00:23,750 --> 00:00:25,712
The core rule is movements.

11
00:00:25,926 --> 00:00:27,782
Stock never changes by editing a number.

12
00:00:28,016 --> 00:00:33,584
Every use, delivery, move and count is a movement, and the quantity on hand is their sum.

13
00:00:33,776 --> 00:00:35,670
Movements are never edited or deleted.

14
00:00:35,815 --> 00:00:43,187
And no movement can take a location below zero, so if Tom tries to use a case that isn't there, he sees: only zero on hand at Van 2.

15
00:00:44,283 --> 00:00:47,035
Claude built it in four phases, one prompt each.

16
00:00:47,397 --> 00:00:48,123
The foundation.

17
00:00:48,421 --> 00:00:51,109
Recording stock, alerts, labels and scanning.

18
00:00:51,365 --> 00:00:52,219
Purchase orders.

19
00:00:52,475 --> 00:00:53,710
Then counts and reports.

20
00:00:53,921 --> 00:00:58,659
After every phase, the acceptance checks ran in a real browser, from fresh seed data.

21
00:00:59,750 --> 00:01:00,646
Here's what it built.

22
00:01:00,945 --> 00:01:07,537
The Items screen lists eight seed items worth one thousand one hundred and thirty-three dollars, with gloves marked low and twenty on order.

23
00:01:07,771 --> 00:01:11,074
The item page shows a move from the store room to a van as one row.

24
00:01:11,219 --> 00:01:16,723
Reorder suggestions offer twenty-four bottles of glass cleaner, but skip the gloves, because they're already on order.

25
00:01:17,065 --> 00:01:19,486
The label sheet prints QR codes three across.

26
00:01:19,816 --> 00:01:23,531
And a count review shows two differences before an admin approves them.

27
00:01:24,617 --> 00:01:31,244
In one final run, on a fresh database, all twenty-one checks passed, along with one hundred and thirty-seven automated tests.

28
00:01:32,350 --> 00:01:34,334
But the more useful result is what broke.

29
00:01:34,654 --> 00:01:38,089
The test build exposed sixty-three places where Claude had to guess.

30
00:01:38,452 --> 00:01:40,843
My own seed history couldn't add up to my seed table.

31
00:01:41,057 --> 00:01:45,575
Nothing tied a reason to a direction, so an adjustment marked Found could remove stock.

32
00:01:45,869 --> 00:01:51,522
And a count waiting for review didn't block a second count, so approving both would correct the same stock twice.

33
00:01:51,885 --> 00:01:53,845
Every gap is now fixed in the spec.

34
00:01:54,950 --> 00:01:57,574
Passing every check isn't the same as production-ready.

35
00:01:57,808 --> 00:02:03,716
We didn't test production sign-in, real email delivery, the daily job on a real scheduler, or hosting.

36
00:02:03,907 --> 00:02:08,942
Codes were typed into the scan screen, not read by a phone camera, and the labels were never printed.

37
00:02:10,050 --> 00:02:11,543
So is it worth building?

38
00:02:11,927 --> 00:02:21,716
Billed monthly, Sortly's Advanced plan is forty-nine dollars a month, Ultra one hundred and forty-nine, and Premium two hundred and ninety-nine, with two, five and eight user licenses.

39
00:02:21,863 --> 00:02:24,295
Purchase orders and stock counts start on Ultra.

40
00:02:24,509 --> 00:02:27,581
If one person tracks fewer than a hundred items, the free plan may be enough.

41
00:02:27,751 --> 00:02:31,624
If more than eight people record stock, or you want counts without paying for Ultra, build it.

42
00:02:32,717 --> 00:02:34,040
Everything is in one file.

43
00:02:34,296 --> 00:02:41,352
The data model, ten rules, seed data, four phase prompts, and all twenty-one checks, with the sixty-three fixes already in.

44
00:02:42,333 --> 00:02:45,492
The full spec is free at personal software factory dot com.

45
00:02:45,542 --> 00:02:47,825
If you build it, tell me what broke.

46
00:02:47,875 --> 00:02:48,408
I'm Sam.

47
00:02:48,458 --> 00:02:49,283
See you tomorrow.

